{%- comment -%}
  Window display — the top of the homepage, laid out like a shop window: the
  most in the least space. Three columns from `lg` (1 : 2 : 1):

  - lead:  a statement panel and one feature card below it
  - hero:  this week's edit, an image or video with its headline and button,
           and three of its products underneath
  - side:  a featured collection, as a short list, and Ask Essie prompts

  Below `lg` the hero comes first, then the statement and feature card side by
  side (stacked on phones), then the collection and Essie. The hero is first in
  the source too, so that is also the reading order and it is the first image
  to load.

  Each part is a static block (blocks/_window-*.liquid): edited on its own in
  the theme editor, but fixed in place, so it can't be removed or moved. This
  file only lays them out.

  Analytics (frontend/scripts/analytics.js): `data-feature` sends
  feature_view when the window is first seen, and any click inside it marks
  `window_display` on the order's `_features`. Each part is a [data-card]
  (hero, statement, feature-card, collection-list) so section_click says
  which one was used; the two product lists are `window_edit` and
  `window_collection`; the Essie prompts send select_content.
{%- endcomment -%}

<div class="window-display" data-feature="window_display">
  {% content_for 'block', type: '_window-hero', id: 'hero' %}

  <div class="window-display__lead">
    {% content_for 'block', type: '_window-statement', id: 'statement' %}
    {% content_for 'block', type: '_window-feature', id: 'feature' %}
  </div>

  <div class="window-display__side">
    {% content_for 'block', type: '_window-collection', id: 'collection' %}
    {% content_for 'block', type: '_window-essie', id: 'essie' %}
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Window Display",
  "class": "section-type--window-display",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Window Display"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
